{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Dead-man coverage for the nine `notBreaching` counters — proven, with its residual named\n\nPushed as Gerrit change **2683**. Before pushing I was asked to prove, not assert, that moving nine\nerror counters to `notBreaching` does not create a blind spot — because under `notBreaching`\n\"the publisher is dead\" and \"there were no errors\" become the same observation, which for an error\ncounter is a check that stops checking exactly when something is wrong.\n\n**The early heartbeats do NOT cover them.** Over the same 8-hour sweep, `mcp_healthy` (§1b) and\n`gate_scratch_mounted` (§2e) were each present in **54 buckets where `g2p_dispatch_errors` was\nabsent**. They do not stop together, because the truncation lands between them. Anyone reasoning\n\"the heartbeats carry the dead-man\" from head-of-script signals is wrong, and the data says so.\n\n**The tail sentinel does cover them.** `mirror_out_of_sync` publishes unconditionally at\nobservability.sh line 1511; all nine counters publish at lines 1193-1463. Publication is\nsequential, so reaching 1511 implies every counter's line was reached — sentinel silence is a\nsuperset of counter silence. Tested per 5-minute bucket:\n\n| counter | line | present | sentinel present while counter ABSENT |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `replication_errors` | 1193 | 49 | 3 |\n| `voter_errors` | 1249 | 51 | 3 |\n| `review_bot_merge_change_errors` | 1273 | 46 | 3 |\n| `deploy_errors` | 1301 | 46 | 3 |\n| `review_interrupts_bound_exceeded` | 1391 | 44 | 2 |\n| `review_interrupts_signal_unavailable` | 1395 | 44 | 2 |\n| `mcp_retire_cap` | 1419 | 43 | 1 |\n| `mcp_mem_abort` | 1421 | 42 | 0 |\n| `g2p_dispatch_errors` | 1463 | 41 | 0 |\n\nThe residual falls monotonically to zero with proximity to the sentinel — what sequential\nexecution predicts and what coincidence would not. Against the early heartbeats' 54, that is two\norders of magnitude.\n\nBecause the ordering is load-bearing, it is **enforced rather than commented**:\n`test_the_tail_sentinel_is_published_after_every_notbreaching_counter` fails the build if\n`mirror_out_of_sync` moves earlier or a counter is added after it. Without that guard, an\ninnocuous reordering of the probe would remove the dead-man from nine alarms with no other symptom.\n\n### Residual, recorded rather than left to be discovered\n\n1. Dead-publisher detection for those nine slows from their own ~15-minute windows to the\n   sentinel's 40-minute one.\n2. All nine now depend on a single alarm — a new coupling, held in place only by the test above.\n3. Up to 3 buckets per counter where the sentinel published and the counter did not: conditional\n   publish paths and bucket-boundary skew, not truncation. In exactly those cases the counter's\n   silence genuinely is not publisher death, which is what `notBreaching` is for.\n4. **Follow-up that retires all three:** `ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` (`9313-1fac-9f32-4b07`) adds\n   purpose-built `probe_ok` (published once, last) and `probe_truncated` (ExecStopPost, which\n   systemd runs even after a timeout SIGTERM) with alarms in `monitoring_9313.tf`. Those report\n   truncation directly instead of inferring it from an absence, and the sentinel duty should move\n   to them when they land.\n\n### Also recorded durably, not just in conversation\n\nThese windows are sized to the probe's **5-9 minute cadence CONTRACT, not to today's 85-minute tail\ngaps**. Sizing to the defect would bake a 105-minute window into production monitoring and outlive\nthe bug it was fitted to. Until `9313` lands, the tail alarms still produce some false pages — just\nstrictly fewer, and none of them unclearable. This ticket's truncation measurement independently\ncorroborates `9313`, which is being fixed in parallel; the two fixes are complementary and neither\nsubstitutes for the other.\n\nChange 2683 is open with `LLM-Review` and `Verified` both pending. Terraform remains unapplied."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788639893590308269,"uuid":"4ac83a05-988a-4088-8421-cf7525673aa8"}